A||N||17||Heisenhund||Idle problems||||8||
Z||000000||Heisenhund||09-03-2000||02:44 PM||||Okay folks, tell me what I'm missing....<P>About a month ago, I changed the plugs in my 86 GT (the front ones were a pain, but I got them OK). A little while later I noticed that my idle speed stays up between 1800 - 2000 RPM. And I'm throwing "35" codes. Hmmmm....IAC bad, okay, get a new one. While I'm waiting for my local parts dealer (Autozone) to get a new one, I search high & low for vacuum leaks. None to be found, but I didn't pull the plenum. New IAC installed, no change. Check the EGR tube for cracks. None that I can find (although I would appreciate some input on other ways to check for that).<P>Bottom line...I'm looking for ideas or "lessons learned" from you folks.<P>Anybody got any?||||reg||8
Z||000001||RedOktober||09-03-2000||02:51 PM||||You have a vaccum leak. Get a can of carb cleaner or brake cleaner and spray around the intake. Had the same problem...Mine was the small rubber hose that goes under the throttle body. It had a crack in it( very small but enough to make the idle bounce.)<P>The ther thing it could be is if the O2 sensor is old. <P>Good luck <P>------------------<BR>86 SE V6 Auto Turbo Going 3.4 OHV<BR>86 2M4 5sp (Beater)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Heisenhund||09-04-2000||07:37 AM||||OK, I feel down in my bones that it really is a vacuum leak. I feel this mainly because I've already spent the $$ for the IAC.<P>So are there any diagrams on the web of the vacuum harness & which hoses go where? The one on the underside of the hood is a bit too faded to follow all the lines.<P>Heis<P>O2 sensor? ECM thinks the mixtures out of whack?||||reg||5
Z||000003||BOILERMAKER||09-04-2000||07:39 PM||||There is no set in stone answer for this.  While it is possible that you have a vac. leak, I wouldn't bet all of my money on that.<P>I had somewhat of the same problem.  Changed plugs, then I had a constant velocity / zero acceleration hesitation (hiccup if you will) that I could not find at all.  I came up with the same code 35 and changed the IAC as well.<P>In the end I changed the wires and coil and the problem was gone.  I'm betting that in my case it was actually the coil.  I changed it out with another coil (from a wreck) and it did the same thing, but when I put on a brand new one, the problem was resolved.<P>I also had another problem with my automatic that would studder whenever it hit the lockup point on the tranny (the point after 3rd gear).  Long story short - I had to tighten the dogbone down.<P>Hope this helps.  That's just my experience.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Heisenhund||09-04-2000||10:04 PM||||Hmmm...<P>If you're talking about the ignition coil, I've been thinking lately that I should've replaced that when I did the plugs. I got this car with 131K on it & no idea what had or had not been done to it before. I do know that none of the recall work has been done.<P>I'll give that a try, because the vacuum leak search ain't going so well.<P>Heis<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000005||Heisenhund||09-05-2000||06:35 PM||||OK, 2 suggestions didn't pan out. I must be missing something obvious. I replaced the O2 sensor, the ignition coil and sprayed enough carb cleaner around the intake that I was afraid I would start a fire. No luck. Anybody else have any other experience with this?? I'm about to just start telling everyone that it idles at 2K because it's a Fiero, most won't know the difference. But it will continue to grate on my teeth until I can figure it out.<P>Heis<BR>||||reg||8
Z||000006||BOILERMAKER||09-05-2000||10:29 PM||||Sorry those hints didn't work out for you.  For a high idle I would look right at the EGR valve and tube, but since you have already checked that out, I'm at a loss.<P>Have you checked for cracks on your exhaust manifold?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phil||09-06-2000||08:12 AM||||Carbon/dirt build-up at the throttle plate?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Heisenhund||09-06-2000||06:48 PM||||Okay, here's a twist.<P>I'm going hand over hand on the vacuum hoses. When I disconnect the hose to the MAP sensor, idle returns to a more normal state. I think "D**n, I thought I checked that". Run over to my favorite parts place (who start entering my vehicle data as soon as I pull in) & get a new one. Install it. Same problem. Same symptom too...when I remove the hose & plug it, idle returns to normal.<P>Anyone out there a MAP expert?<P>Heis||||reg||5
Z||000009||ROB MAC||09-06-2000||10:25 PM||||I just went through idle problems ; first it was high idle that would stick while driving , then it was jumpy idle up and down . All symptoms dissappeared after I disconnected the faulty headlight isolation relay. Now idle is rock steady.||||reg||1
